<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?><ns2:section xmlns:ns2="http://rest.cis.illinois.edu" id="52373" href="schedule/2020/spring/SPAN/467/52373.xml"><parents><calendarYear id="2020" href="schedule/2020.xml">2020</calendarYear><term id="120201" href="schedule/2020/spring.xml">Spring 2020</term><subject id="SPAN" href="schedule/2020/spring/SPAN.xml">Spanish</subject><course id="467" href="schedule/2020/spring/SPAN/467.xml">19thC Sp American Studies</course></parents><sectionNumber>U3 </sectionNumber><creditHours>3 hours</creditHours><statusCode>A</statusCode><sectionText>In the wake of Latin American independence, writers and artists forged new ways of representing Latin American identities. In this course, we will read a variety of foundational texts from the nineteenth century that revise ideas about race, gender, and politics in Latin America. These texts grapple with Latin America's place in the world, as well as timely issues such as slavery, racial mixing, democracy, and the role of women in society.</sectionText><sectionNotes>Restricted to Undergrad - Urbana-Champaign.</sectionNotes><partOfTerm>1</partOfTerm><sectionStatusCode>A</sectionStatusCode><enrollmentStatus>UNKNOWN</enrollmentStatus><startDate>2020-01-21Z</startDate><endDate>2020-05-06Z</endDate><meetings><meeting id="0"><type code="LCD">Lecture-Discussion</type><start>02:00 PM</start><end>03:20 PM</end><daysOfTheWeek>TR     </daysOfTheWeek><roomNumber>1126</roomNumber><buildingName>Foreign Languages Building</buildingName><instructors><instructor lastName="Fornoff" firstName="C">Fornoff, C</instructor></instructors></meeting></meetings></ns2:section>